TEST 16452 -- loop-protection action: port disable
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-24 16:51-16:52 NZST, tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk"
Setup:  the same loop as 16453.log (same session): sa2's two legs out of both LAGs into an
        empty vlan 60, so the x230 bridges DUT port1.0.2 <-> port1.0.9.
        (Replaces the 2026-09-22 UNMEASURED "port-disable is not supported with fast
        blocking".)

VERDICT: PASS -- on a real loop the port is logically blocked while the link stays up at
         both ends.

PRECONDITION -- clearing fast-block
  `loop-protection action port-disable` is refused while fast-block is on ("% Port-disable
  action is not supported with fast blocking"). On 2026-09-22 fast-block looked sticky:
  `no loop-protection loop-detect fast-block` is "% Invalid input", and re-issuing the
  command without the keyword does not clear it. The way that works:
      no loop-protection loop-detect
      loop-protection loop-detect ldf-interval 1        (no fast-block)
  Then port-disable is accepted on port1.0.2/1.0.9 (timeout 60). The baseline (with
  fast-block) was restored afterwards.

STEP -- "Set loop-protection action to port disable. Create a loop between two ports."
        -> "The port will be logically disabled to prevent traffic flowing, but the link will
           remain up (the device at the other end will not notice ...)"
  loop closed 16:51:15 (x230 port1.0.4 no shutdown). Reads +16 s to +47 s, every ~6 s:
      DUT  show loop-protection:  port1.0.2  port-dis  Blocking  timeout 60, remain 54..24,
                                  Rx port port1.0.9   (port1.0.9 Normal)
      DUT  show interface status: port1.0.2  connected   port1.0.9 connected
      x230 show interface status: port1.0.3  connected   port1.0.4 connected
  -> port1.0.2 is Blocking (logically disabled) while its link, and the x230's end of it,
     stays connected. The contrast with 16453 (err-disable / far end notconnect, same loop)
     is the evidence that the two actions differ as the cases describe.

NOTE: loop-protection cannot be configured on an aggregator member port ("% ... Loop
  detection can not be configured on aggregator member ports"), which is why the legs come
  out of sa2 for the test.

TEARDOWN: as 16453.log.
